This new environment has other resources and other competitors, so in order to be a strong competitor, the new population needs different features or adaptations than they needed before.
Indispensators haven't changed at all, they still need the same adjustments as before.
Over time, when the new population begins to adapt to their new environment, they begin to look smaller and less like the second population.
Gradually, after thousands or even millions of years, the two populations will look so different they can't be summoned the same species.
We call this process specification, which simply means the formation of new species. Specization is an unavoidable consequence and a very important part of evolution.
